<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doria family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Doria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Doria were one of the great noble families of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, prominent from the 12th century. Doria admirals won victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> at Meloria in 1284 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> at Curzola in 1298, and the family held lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Its most famous member, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a>, reshaped Genoa as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1528.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doria-family.jpg" alt="Doria family" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_House_of_Doria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from de Auria, from de filiis Auriae, the sons of Auria, later de Oria or d'Oria. According to legend a noble Genoese lady, Auria or Oria della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>, loved a pilgrim bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, Arduino di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, and their children took their mother's name. Some have speculated that the family descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a> family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piemonte</a>, some of whom were Counts of Auriate. It appears suddenly as a major local power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> in the late 11th century, and documents of 1110 name two members, Martino and Genuardo, as filii Auriae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from the 12th to the 15th century and at Dolceacqua, Oneglia and Portofino. Simon Doria was an admiral of the Genoese at the crusaders' assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in the late 12th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria">Oberto Doria</a>, Captain of the People, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>'s fleet against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> at Meloria, and his brother Lamba defeated the Venetian Andrea Dandolo at Curzola. Tedisio Doria financed the voyage of Vadino and Ugolino Vivaldi in 1291, and Branca Doria appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Divine Comedy for murdering his father-in-law, Michele Zanche. Brancaleone Doria ruled the Giudicato of Arborea and nearly conquered all Sardinia in the late 14th century.</p><p>Ottone Doria commanded the Genoese crossbowmen hired by the French at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in August 1346 and was killed there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a>, became perpetual censor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1528 and admiral to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>; his titles passed to Giovanni Andrea Doria, son of his adopted son Giannettino, who was killed in the Fieschi conspiracy of 1547. Members of the family served as doge from Giovanni Battista Doria in 1537 to Giuseppe Maria Doria in 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Dorias helped finance the Portuguese and Spanish voyages: Francesco Doria, a banker at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, financed the expeditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, and his granddaughter Clemenza Doria was among the earliest settlers of Portuguese Brazil. Branches bore the titles of Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> and Prince of Centola and many lesser ones, and some male children were named after foreign rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazan-khan">Ghazan Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hethum-i">Hethum I</a> of Cilician Armenia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Doria (family)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the time Sardinia was divided between the Republic of Pisa, which held Cagliari and the south-west with its silver mines, Genoese noble families such as the <b>Doria</b> in the north, and the native Judicate of Arborea in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was dominated by great clans, the <b>Doria</b>, Spinola, Fieschi and Grimaldi among them, organised from the fourteenth century in alberghi, associations of related families sharing a name and a neighbourhood.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The realm was then carved up between the <b>Doria</b> and Malaspina families, the Judicate of Arborea and the town of Sassari, which became the Republic of Sassari.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-logudoro">Judicate of Logudoro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-house"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Rulers and members</h2><figure class="pp-chart"><p class="cty-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>1200</span><span>1400</span><span>1600</span></p><ol class="cty-tl pp-tl"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Oberto Doria</span><span class="cty-y">13th century</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:0.0%;width:24.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lamba-doria"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Lamba Doria</span><span class="cty-y">1245–1323</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:11.3%;width:19.5%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/andrea-doria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Andrea Doria</span><span class="cty-y">1466–1560</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:66.5%;width:23.5%"></span></span></li></ol><figcaption>3 people of the encyclopedia whose Wikidata record names this house as their family. Bright bars are lifespans; pale bars reigns or years of activity.</figcaption></figure><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynasties">All ruling houses &rarr;</a></p></section>
